Michael Kantor: And these specifications include things like, how do you store files on-chain? For example, HCS1, how do you create a decentralized registry? How do you recurse through files? How do you have agents talking to each other? And so forth.

162
00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:25.509
Michael Kantor: At a wider lens, these specifications are essentially metaprotocols. If you're familiar with ecosystems like ordinals on Bitcoin, or if you're familiar with things like JSON schemas, that's pretty much all HCS specs are. They're application-level

163
00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:35.980
Michael Kantor: JSON schemas that have immutable, agreed-upon patterns for doing certain things, like file storage and registration of data.

164
00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:52.050
Michael Kantor: It's built entirely on the Hedera Consensus Service, although it will also utilize services like the account service, using the memo field, and so forth, or sometimes the HCS service, in the case of, standards and specifications, like Hassionals, right?

165
00:24:52.050 --> 00:25:01.060
Michael Kantor: So it goes well with these other services and enables application-level patterns that are really important for users in day-to-day,

166
00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:04.449
Michael Kantor: Things like file storage and registration and so forth.

167
00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:20.780
Michael Kantor: As of today, we have over 21 standards that are already built. Not all of these standards are published, many of them are still in a draft status, which means that they're being vetted. We're seeing what works and what doesn't work, and when they are ready, then they go into a published state, which basically says.

168
00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:27.449
Michael Kantor: We've given a rubber stamp and said that, okay, this specification makes sense, it's ready to be used in production.

169
00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:35.930
Michael Kantor: That's all the statuses mean, but for all intents and purposes, anyone can contribute an HCS specification, and

170
00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:41.359
Michael Kantor: They can have their draft merged into the repository, so it can be shared and visible.

171
00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:50.420
Michael Kantor: So you're probably wondering, what are some examples of HCS specifications? One of our most popular ones is HCS1.

172
00:25:50.480 --> 00:26:02.350
Michael Kantor: which devised a clever way of storing files on graph. So, let's say, for example, and you might have seen this presentation at Hedericon, actually. This has been a little bit revised.

173
00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:11.029
Michael Kantor: Let's say you have a 4KB JPEG, and you were to take that 4KB JPEG, and you were to split it into 4 little pieces.

174
00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:19.100
Michael Kantor: You're probably wondering why 4 pieces? Well, if you're familiar with the consensus service and HIRA in general, you have that one kilobyte limit.

175
00:26:19.390 --> 00:26:35.810
Michael Kantor: for putting, data onto HCS. So we take that for kilobytes and break it down for little pieces, we compress those pieces, and then we put them on as messages to a, IRO consensus topic.

176
00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:46.099
Michael Kantor: In that topic, we include a memo field, which has some information about the data that has been inscribed, and inside an application, let's say a wallet.

177
00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:52.489
Michael Kantor: Or front-end, it basically pulls down those messages and then reconstructs them after decompressing them.

178
00:26:52.570 --> 00:27:10.000
Michael Kantor: So, with every CS specification, there is a kind of JSON schema that comes along with it that is formatted and agreed upon. Generally, with these schemas, we try to keep the field names very short, because we're cognizant of how little space we have in these 1KB

179
00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.630
Michael Kantor: messages. So, with HCS1,

180
00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:30.079
Michael Kantor: We have two fields, O and C. O is the order, and C is the actual content that has been compressed for that message. The clever aspect of this is the O field, which allows you to, for example, say you have a 1GB file.

181
00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:41.600
Michael Kantor: You can create a 1GB worth of chunks, and you can inscribe them or put them on the topic in parallel, meaning you can have thousands of transactions going out.

182
00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:58.640
Michael Kantor: At the same time, so you don't have to wait for a sequential transaction approval, which would be very, very slow and tedious. And then on the application level, you simply order by the O field so that you have that final, image file, and you can reconstruct it the same way.

183
00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:13.149
Michael Kantor: It was a week or so ago, somebody recently put out a 500 megabytes of data, and it was inscribed within, 6 or 8 hours, or something like that, which was very cool.

